  "query_preview": "..\n\nPretty quiet forecast heading through the end of the work week as \nthe energetic jet stream resides mostly across Canada. Stacked low \ncentered over Hudson Bay today will send a trough down through the \nGreat Lakes on the nose of a mid level jet streak tonight. This \nopens the door for broad troughing to encompass a portion of the \nnorthern Great Lakes while high pressure slides through the Midwest \ninto the Ohio Valley building a stable airmass across the southern \nGreat Lakes. For tonight, northwesterly cool flow over the \nrelatively warm lakes will provide a boost to instability and though \nmoisture is weak, PWATs roughly 0.6 inches, there could be enough \nconcentrated moisture in the region of steeper low/mid level lapse \nrates to allow some scattered showers or even a thunderstorm over \nthe north half of Lake Huron. Further south into the Thumb, moisture \nis even thinner and forcing weaker, but the cool airmass and low \nlevel jet could be enough to touch off some scattered showers over \nthe Thumb this evening into tonight. Current radar over the U.P. \ndoes have thunderstorms present adding to the confidence of weak \nstorms over northern Huron later. \n\nHigh pressure then expands back northward across the region ending \nany rain chances Thursday into Friday. Cool air within the trough \nwill drop 850mb temps into the single digits above 0C leading to \ncool highs in the 60s to end the week. Clearing skies under the \nridge with the cool airmass in place could allow some low temps to \nfall into the upper 30s Thursday night in some of the cooler spots, \notherwise low 40s could be in store for most locations. \n\nBrief but strong warm up on Saturday as strong ridging over the \nPlains advances eastward over the Great Lakes. ECMWF is a touch \nwarmer (~18C at 850mb) than GFS (-16C) Saturday with strong \nsouthwesterly flow into the region. Current forecast is for temps to \ntouch 80F in the Saginaw Valley with upper 70s for most other \nlocations. The warm front leading the way could touch off a few \nshowers after 00Z Saturday with the bubble of stronger theta e \nlifting through the area. Though model output is not strong in \nproducing rain, we've see these leading theta e gradients on \nelevated warm fronts produce narrow brief areas of showers many \ntimes. For now, we'll note it and see if we need to add POPs later. \n\nThis warmup will be brief as attention the turns to TS Isaias which \nis set to make landfall over MS Friday evening then getting drawn \nnorthward into the Ohio Valley before getting caught in the \nwesterlies and forced to the east coast. This brings the rain shield \ninto Lower MI Saturday night and/or Sunday. Details will come \ntogether in the coming days, but for now expect a gradient of rain \nfrom south to north with the highest totals over southern lower MI. \nAbundant moisture with a tropical system will lead to efficient rain \nproduction for any areas that get clipped by the rain shield.",
